Currently, there seem to be two primary types of thief behaviors (not
including the ability of major baddies to steal artifacts).  Nymphs
and leps steal and teleport, then after a while of being scared resume
trying to steal.  Monkeys steal and run away, but are usually not
difficult to hunt down to retrieve the item stolen.

The proposed new behavior:
A thief that attempts to steal an item and try to escape to another
dungeon level.  Once out of sight of the player (I'm thinking they
should often be generated with a scroll of teleportation to facilitate
this), the would try to find either the up or down stairs and use
them.  If successful, they'd get placed in a random position on the
new level (to simulate the time the monster has had to make himself
scarce).  When the player follows onto that level, the thief resumes
trying to escape via the stairs again, over and over.  The monster
doesn't resume trying to steal.
Effect on game: This makes thief monsters a little more "aware" of the
existance of other levels in the dungeon, and also gives them a
strategy as to what to do after the player's hard-won loot is in their
hands.  It makes it possible to lose an item for a significant amount
of time, especially if the player doesn't see the thief before he
escapes on the next level.

This is a lot more complicated, but ultimately it'd be nice if the
thief tried to sell his loot at dungeon shops.  I don't even want to
speculate how hard that would be to implement, however.

